+# UPDATE RUNBOOK — how this fork moves forward
+
+This file is the O(delta) recipe for keeping the ArifiLabs llama.cpp fork current. It is written to
+be executed **verbatim by any model or engineer**, including one that has never seen this repository
+before. Nothing here requires taste. If a step needs a judgment call, the step says so and stops.
+
+Three things can make the fork stale, and they are different jobs:
+
+| trigger | job | section |
+|---|---|---|
+| upstream `ggml-org/llama.cpp` tagged something newer | **upstream bump** | §2 |
+| a tracked fork moved past its reviewed pin | **fork ingest** | §3 |
+| neither — you just changed something here | **verify** | §4 |
+
+The one thing that is true of all three: **`arifi/main` is the only canonical branch, and it stays
+linear.** Never merge into it. A merge commit is not a style preference here — `git format-patch`
+omits merge commits by construction, so a merge's hand-made conflict resolutions never reach
+`patches/series/`, and the next replay dies on the first commit that builds on them. That is not a
+prediction; it happened, it was measured at patch 0081, and flattening the history is what fixed it.
+
+---
+
+## 0. Vocabulary, in one table
+
+| term | what it means here |
+|---|---|
+| `arifi/main` | THE canonical branch. Everything ships from it. Zero merges. |
+| the base | the upstream commit `arifi/main` sits on: `tools/arifi-sync/sources.json` → `base.upstream_sha` |
+| the series | `patches/series/` — a **generated** mirror of `base..arifi/main`. Never hand-edited. |
+| a pin | a reviewed sha of a tracked third-party fork, in `sources.json` → `remotes` / `local_checkouts` |
+| BEHIND | a pin's fork has commits past the pin. Currency reports it; it is a queue item, not an alarm. |
+| a lane worktree | a temporary `git worktree` for one piece of work. Reaped at integration. See §5. |
+
+## 1. Before anything: know where you are
+
+```bash
+cd <repo>
+python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py status
+```
+
+`status` prints the base, the series state, provenance coverage and currency in one shot. Read it
+before you decide which of §2/§3/§4 you are in. Every `arifi_sync` subcommand exits 0 **only** on a
+full pass; a non-zero exit is a stop, never a warning to route around.
+
+## 2. Upstream bump (the common case)
+
+### 2.1 One command, if it works
+
+```bash
+python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py bump --onto <new-upstream-sha>
+```
+
+`bump` runs replay + build + judge and **refuses unless all three pass**. When it passes, you are
+done except for §4.3 and the register flip.
+
+### 2.2 When `bump` refuses — the manual path
+
+`bump`'s failure names the patch, the file and the hunk. Do not guess at the rest.
+
+```bash
+# 1. fetch upstream and pick the target
+git fetch upstream
+git log --oneline -5 upstream/master
+
+# 2. dry-run the series onto the new base, in a scratch worktree, touching nothing
+python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py series replay --onto <new-upstream-sha>
+```
+
+If the replay is clean, move the branch and regenerate:
+
+```bash
+git switch arifi/main
+git rebase --onto <new-upstream-sha> <old-base-sha> arifi/main
+#   ^ if this reports conflicts the replay lied; STOP and re-read the replay output.
+
+# update the base in sources.json: base.upstream_tag, base.upstream_sha  (base.ref stays "arifi/main")
+python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py series regen
+python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py series check     # must exit 0
+```
+
+If the replay is **not** clean, resolve one patch at a time in the scratch worktree, and record why
+in the commit body. A resolution that is not explained is a resolution that will be re-broken.
+
+Two rules that are not negotiable at this step:
+
+- **Never `git rebase -i` and never squash.** The 1:1 correspondence between a commit and a patch
+  file is what makes `series check` meaningful.
+- **Never hand-edit a file under `patches/series/`.** It is generated. If it is wrong, the branch is
+  wrong; fix the branch and regenerate.
+
+## 3. Fork ingest (a tracked source moved past its pin)
+
+```bash
+python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py currency
+```
+
+Currency fetches every registered source and reports drift against the reviewed pins. A **BEHIND**
+row is what triggers this section. Handle **one source at a time**.
+
+```bash
+# 1. see exactly what moved — pin..head is the entire review surface
+git -C <source-checkout> log --oneline <pin>..<head>
+
+# 2. review it. Three verdicts, and only three:
+#    TAKE    - a real mechanism we want -> continue
+#    SKIP    - not applicable / not wanted -> re-pin with the reason, done
+#    UNKNOWN - you cannot tell -> re-pin is NOT allowed; leave BEHIND and say so
+```
+
+Before believing a patch series is valuable, **open the source tree's root**. A high clean-apply
+rate measures collision, not value: a package with no `ggml/` and no `src/llama.cpp` can apply 57 of
+57 patches here and add zero engine code. That exact mistake was made, acted on, and reverted.
+
+To TAKE:
+
+```bash
+git switch -c ingest/<source>-<date> arifi/main
+# apply the mechanism as ONE commit per coherent change, each carrying:
+#   Taken-from: <source>@<sha>
+#   Origin:     <what we did to it and why>
+#   Measured-effect: <a number, or the literal word UNMEASURED>
+```
+
+Then: retain the source's `LICENSE`/`NOTICE` in `licenses/` and add its row to
+`licenses/README.md` **before** the code lands — see that file's audit table for what a gap looks
+like. If the source has no license file, say so; never manufacture one.
+
+Then rebase onto `arifi/main`, fast-forward, re-pin the source in `sources.json`, and run §4.
+
+## 4. Verify — the part nobody may skip
+
+### 4.1 The series is honest
+
+```bash
+python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py series regen
+python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py series check      # regen to temp + byte-compare. exit 0.
+python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py series replay --onto <base-sha>
+python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py provenance        # trailer coverage over the whole series
+git rev-list --merges --count <base-sha>..arifi/main    # MUST be 0
+```
+
+### 4.2 It builds — the THREE-flag configure
+
+Windows / MinGW / Vulkan, the only configuration any number in this repository was measured on:
+
+```powershell
+cmake -S . -B build-vulkan -G Ninja `
+  -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release `
+  -DGGML_VULKAN=ON `
+  -DGGML_ARIFI_ROCMFPX_FORMATS=ON `
+  -DGGML_ARIFI_TURBO_WEIGHT_QUANTS=ON `
+  -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS="-D_WIN32_WINNT=0x0A00" `
+  -DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS="-D_WIN32_WINNT=0x0A00"
+
+cmake --build build-vulkan -j2 --target llama-cli llama-server llama-quantize `
+                                        test-backend-ops test-quantize-fns
+```
+
+Three flags, and all three are load-bearing:
+
+- `GGML_ARIFI_ROCMFPX_FORMATS` — without it the six ROCmFPX types do not compile and a ROCmFP4 GGUF
+  is refused at load.
+- `GGML_ARIFI_TURBO_WEIGHT_QUANTS` — without it `TQ3_1S`/`TQ4_1S` and the whole tq3 family at 48-51
+  do not compile.
+- `-D_WIN32_WINNT=0x0A00` in **both** `C` and `CXX` flags — without it the Windows IOCP path fails
+  to compile. One of the two is not enough; the failure is in whichever language you left out.
+
+`-j2` and not more: the Vulkan shader generation step is memory-hungry and a wider build dies on a
+box with unified memory.
+
+**Verify the binaries by mtime, never by the wrapper's exit code.** A build tool can exit 0 having
+relinked nothing:
+
+```powershell
+Get-ChildItem build-vulkan\bin\llama-*.exe | Select-Object Name, LastWriteTime, Length
+```
+
+### 4.3 It is still correct — the guards
+
+```bash
+build-vulkan/bin/test-backend-ops.exe          # the sweep. baseline: 26213/26260 passing.
+build-vulkan/bin/test-quantize-fns.exe
+python ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/arifi_sync_check.py   # CPU/GLSL mirror parity, build-time
+```
+
+Three guards run inside that sweep and all three must stay green:
+
+1. **Build-time constant check** (`arifi_sync_check.py`) — every CPU/GLSL mirrored constant, LUT
+   pair, FA type-id and block size must be registered. An unregistered mirror FAILS.
+2. **Coverage-parity assert** — a type in `all_types[]` with no executed case FAILS. A run that
+   executes zero cases FAILS. This exists because a family with no kernel at all once hid inside a
+   green sweep by declining every case.
+3. **Per-op × type zero-coverage tally** — names every op whose cases were ALL declined.
+
+The two known-flaky bands at the baseline are the DIV f16 band and `q2_0_g128`. Anything else that
+newly fails is a regression, not flake.
+
+### 4.4 It runs — smoke, on the built binaries
+
+Launch one model at a time, reap between runs, and always with the GPU-residency floor set:
+
+```powershell
+$env:ARIFI_GPU_RESIDENT_FLOOR_GB = "7.0"
+build-vulkan\bin\llama-cli.exe --version
+build-vulkan\bin\llama-cli.exe -m <a-model>.gguf -dev Vulkan0 -ngl 999 -p "The capital of France is" -n 24
+```
+
+Read the residency banner, not just the text. Coherent output from a CPU fallback proves the model
+loaded, not that the GPU path works.
+
+### 4.5 Then, and only then, stage and flip
+
+Staging is a **copy from a verified build of canonical**, never a build from a lane branch:
+
+1. copy the built exes into a new `models/engines/<name>/` directory;
+2. re-run §4.4's smoke **from the staged copies** — a staged engine that cannot launch from its own
+   directory is a staged engine that does not exist;
+3. point the state register at it and rewrite the "do not use on" caveat to the new truth.
+
+## 5. The standard this repository is organized to
+
+1. **One canonical branch, `arifi/main`.** It is linear and it has zero merge commits.
+2. **Lane worktrees are temporary.** Work happens in a `git worktree`, the work lands on
+   `arifi/main`, and the worktree is **reaped at integration**. Branches are kept — they are the
+   history — but a worktree that outlives its lane is a second source of truth.
+3. **Staging only from canonical**, and only after §4 passes end to end.
+4. **The series plus the pins are the update path.** The series carries us forward over upstream;
+   the pins carry us forward over the forks. There is no third mechanism, and adding one would mean
+   a bump has somewhere to silently not reach.
+5. **Generated artifacts are never hand-edited** — `patches/series/` above all.
+
+## 6. What this runbook has NOT been executed through
+
+Stated plainly so nobody reads a written procedure as an exercised one: **the first upstream bump
+through §2 has not been run.** This runbook was written at integration time from the mechanisms as
+they exist, and §2.1's `bump` path in particular inherits `arifi-sync`'s own `build` and `judge`
+legs, which that tool's README already marks unexercised. The first real bump is the test of this
+document, and whoever runs it should fix this file where it is wrong rather than working around it.
